# Implementation Approach

Work like a senior developer on this codebase: framework-first, copy existing features, keep route files thin.

## 1. Understand the request

- Identify whether the work is design-system, entity grid, JSON schema form, modal routing, or API wiring
- Prefer existing `@design-system` / `@entity-framework` / `@json-schema-form` primitives
- Ask Neil when product rules conflict (do not guess permissions or copy)

## 2. Plan before coding

Cover:

- Models and API routes (`apiRoutes` entries)
- Data provider + update service
- Grid and/or schema form UI
- App Router pages + `@modal` interceptors if needed
- Permissions / read-only behaviour

## 3. Implementation order

1. Types / models
2. `apiRoutes` + data providers
3. Design-system usage (or new DS component if genuinely shared)
4. Feature hooks and components
5. Routes / modal parallel routes
6. Client schema validation wiring (`schemaDrivenValidation`) when forms are schema-backed
7. Smoke with `npx tsc --noEmit`

## 4. Standing forms rule

- Backend builds JSON Schema (forms builder); UI fetches it
- Client validates against that schema as the user edits (no hand-written field rules that duplicate the schema)
- Server-dependent checks (uniqueness, cross-record rules) run only on submit and surface API errors

## 5. Safety / repo hygiene

- Do not `git init` this folder
- Do not edit `.env*` or `environment.ts` defaults
- Do not create a `Grok/` (or other agent) folder — use `.cursor/rules` + `AGENTS.md`
